advanced/custom-themes.content:
## Override semantic roles

```tsx
import { LuminescentProvider, Button } from "luminescent";

export function Brand() {
  return (
    <LuminescentProvider theme="light" style={{
      "--lumi-color": "135, 190, 255",
      "--lumi-on-accent": "#142239",
      "--lumi-radius-full": "0.75rem",
      "--lumi-focus": "#234f86"
    }}>
      <Button>Our brand</Button>
    </LuminescentProvider>
  );
}
```

Provider style uses the exported LuminescentStyle type, which accepts --lumi- variables alongside CSSProperties. Component style overrides win over defaults.

## Keep a complete palette

Adjust muted text, borders, focus and status colors along with the accent. Test regular/disabled/hover/focus states in both themes. Glow is decorative and should never be the sole way to recognize a state.

## Separate products on one page

Use one provider per independently themed area. Portalled dialogs and menus use that area's portal root, so they inherit custom properties rather than unexpectedly returning to page defaults.

foundations/themes.content:
## Scoped React configuration

```tsx
import { LuminescentProvider, Button } from "luminescent";

export function Themes() {
  return (
    <>
      <LuminescentProvider theme="dark" accent="peach"><Button>Dark workspace</Button></LuminescentProvider>
      <LuminescentProvider theme="light" accent="violet"><Button>Light workspace</Button></LuminescentProvider>
    </>
  );
}
```

The provider adds a scoped wrapper, configures motion, and keeps portalled overlays inside the same themed tree. Nest providers to reset a subtree to another theme. Defaults are dark, peach, normal glow and system motion.

## CSS-only configuration

You can style static content without React context:

```css
.project-panel {
  --lumi-color: 130, 180, 255;
  --lumi-radius-lg: 1.5rem;
  --lumi-glow-strength: 0.4;
}
```

Use class lumi-theme plus data-lumi-theme="light", "dark" or "system" for standalone theme containers. Portalled components need LuminescentProvider to retain a local theme.

## Predictable rendering

System mode uses prefers-color-scheme in CSS. It does not read localStorage during render, rewrite the document, or produce a different server tree. Applications may persist a user choice in a cookie and pass it as a prop.

frameworks.content:
## Vite

Import `luminescent/style.css` in `main.tsx` and components from `luminescent`. Tailwind is not required. Both the package and its dependencies support tree shaking.

## Next.js App Router

Import the stylesheet in `app/layout.tsx`. The library entry includes a client boundary; state and event callbacks belong in a consumer client component. Props crossing the server boundary must remain serializable.

```tsx
"use client";
import { useState } from "react";
import { Toggle } from "luminescent";

export function Notifications() {
  const [checked, setChecked] = useState(false);
  return <Toggle label="Notifications" checked={checked} onChange={setChecked} />;
}
```

## Other SSR frameworks

Import the stylesheet in the application root and render components normally. Avoid random IDs or browser preference checks during render. The provider's system theme uses CSS media queries, so server and initial client markup agree.

## CommonJS

The root exposes a require condition. Stylesheets still belong in your bundler's CSS pipeline. The React server renderer can import and render the packed CJS package; see the compatibility validation in the repository.

getting-started.content:
## Install

Use an application running React 18 or React 19. Install the package with npm:

```bash
npm install luminescent
```

React and React DOM are peer dependencies. Motion, icons, syntax highlighting, and Radix foundations are regular dependencies; no separate peer installation is necessary. Consumers do not need Tailwind CSS.

## Import the stylesheet

Import the stylesheet once at your application's entry point. It contains namespaced component styles and `--lumi-*` variables. It does not install an application reset.

```tsx
import { Button } from "luminescent";
import "luminescent/style.css";

export function App() {
  return <Button>Create project</Button>;
}
```

## Add a theme

Dark styling is the default. Use a provider for scoped themes, accent colors, reduced motion and portal inheritance.

```tsx
import { LuminescentProvider, Button } from "luminescent";

export function App() {
  return (
    <LuminescentProvider theme="system" accent="violet" glow="subtle">
      <Button>Create project</Button>
    </LuminescentProvider>
  );
}
```

## Next steps

Read [quick start](/docs/quick-start), [Next.js integration](/docs/advanced/ssr), or [theming](/docs/foundations/themes). The repository currently retains package version 0.1.7; unreleased changes are described in the changelog and require a new release before they are available from npm.

quick-start.content:
## Compose a small form

Checkbox, TextField and Select use native input events. Slider and Rating use numeric callbacks; Toggle and Accordion use boolean callbacks. This distinction preserves existing APIs.

```tsx
import { Button, Checkbox, TextField } from "luminescent";
import "luminescent/style.css";

export function Signup() {
  return (
    <form onSubmit={event => {
      event.preventDefault();
      const values = new FormData(event.currentTarget);
      console.log(values.get("email"));
    }}>
      <TextField label="Email" type="email" name="email" autoComplete="email" required />
      <Checkbox label="Send project updates" name="updates" defaultChecked />
      <Button type="submit">Join the workspace</Button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Add feedback deliberately

Use inline error messages for fields. Alert announces an application status; Progress describes long-running work. A Toast can confirm a user action without replacing essential information.

## Keep layout in your application

Luminescent does not reset your CSS or impose a page layout. Use CSS grid/flexbox, application spacing, and semantic landmarks around the components. All components support className and style on their documented element.
